  "context_pill": "Diversion vs Application of Professional Income",
  "example_summary": "Mr. Bhargava received professional income of 5 lacs and created a trust. The test of diversion by overriding title versus application of income determines whether the amount is taxable.",
  "source_provision": "| Legal Concept | Statutory Basis | Tax Consequence |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Diversion by Overriding Title | Section 4 | Income is diverted before accrual; not taxable in hands of recipient. |\n| Application of Income | Section 5 | Income is earned/accrued first; taxable in hands of recipient. |\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Diversion by Overriding Title: The legal obligation must exist prior to the receipt of income, creating a charge on the income such that the assessee has no right to receive it.\n\n> [!WARNING] Application of Income: When an assessee receives income and subsequently decides to apply it for a specific purpose (charity, trust, personal debt), it remains assessable in their hands as professional or business income.",
  "director": {
    "archetype": "two_regime_comparison",
    "justification": "The case hinges on distinguishing between diversion by overriding title and application of income to determine taxability of professional fees.",
